Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  there's been  a tremendous revival of pilgrimage in  recent years  in europe and there's always been lots  of pilgrimages to mecca  to in india in iran there are huge  pilgrimages with millions of people  walking from one shrine to another but  in europe which is very secular part of  the world  um pilgrimage more or less died out  in the 20th century there were still  local pilgrimages in catholic countries  but  um as a huge phenomenon which it is in  many parts of the world  it didn't really exist in in where it  used to in the middle ages  but the revival has been dramatic in  1987 when the  route to santiago de compostela was  reopened with places for pilgrims to  stay  refugios and the infrastructure for  walking there  um 1 000 people walked there in 1987.  it was soon after it had been  reestablished  last year 2019 330  000 people walked to santiago de  compostela  some also went by bicycle and some went  on horseback  so there's been this tremendous growth  and here in britain  the british pilgrimage trust is  reopening  the old way from southampton or  winchester to canterbury 19 days walking  over the south dans and through  extraordinary beautiful scenery and  this year the british pilgrimage trust  launched 42 new one-day pilgrimage  routes  to every one of the cathedrals in  england  when i say new i mean recent  recently reopened i mean these are  ancient sites of pilgrimage  um but they've been re-established and  now you can walk there  and get do five or six mile walk  on a short pilgrimage this is actually a  really nice way of doing a pilgrimage  i myself uh do one of these one day  pilgrimages  every year i have a teenage godson and  when he was 14 i couldn't think  what to give him i don't give stuff  anymore i give experiences and  so because i was interested in  pilgrimage i offered him  um a present her birthday present in  june when his birthday is  of walking together on a pilgrimage to  canterbury  through the fields on footpaths through  meadows  uh through woods and orchards um  to canterbury cathedral i didn't know  whether he'd want to do that or not but  he  loved the idea and we had the most  wonderful day  um we arrived at the theater we let  candles of the shrine of saint thomas  beckett  we had a cream tea and we went to coral  even song this beautiful  song service that happens every day in  the english cathedrals  you don't have to be a paid up  practicing christian to go to this  it's open to everyone and indeed  the british pilgrimage trust when it  leads guided pilgrimages  has as one of its slogans bring your own  beliefs  the people going on these pilgrimages um  are certainly not all  totally devout christians or members of  other religions  a lot of people are agnostic or  spiritual but not religious and i think  it's something that  for many people is a physical expression  of a spiritual quest if you're on a  spiritual journey  then actually making it real by walking  to a holy place is a very very good way  of of doing it so there's a wide range  of choice and  these include pilgrimages to the sources  of rivers  to ancient trees and holy wells  to pre-question holy places and of  course  also to great christian holy places like  medieval churches  ruined monasteries and  cathedrals the basis  of pilgrimage i think is very deep  archetypal i think that's one reason  it's taking off so much in in modern  europe  um the deep archetype i think comes from  the  fact that our ancestors were  hunter-gatherers  hunter-gatherers can't just stay in one  place so  animals don't come to them to be hunted  and fruit and nuts and  and roots and so on don't just drop into  their laps they have to  go around the landscape in accordance  with the changing seasons  as they do so they go to places of  significance with stories and  those are the kind of primal ancestral  holy places they visit  in australia the australian aborigines  sing the stories of these places on  their journey in what are called song  lines  they tell the story they give meaning to  the journey and the places  and they have a history now i think when  the neolithic revolution occurred and  people settled down  in particular places then  they still had this urge to go together  at sacred places stonehenge for example  here in england was not at the center of  a city  like temples where in sumeria and  babylonia and  egypt it wasn't in an urban type setting  rather it was in the middle of the  countryside and people would have had to  go there  for festivals probably at the solstices  and maybe at other times of year  this archetype is found in every  religious tradition  and it's all over the world i lived in  india for seven years where i worked in  a  international agricultural institute  there's a  tremendous amount of pilgrimage in india  there are pilgrimages  among muslims in india many muslims go  to the shrines of sufi saints on  pilgrimages called daggers  and of course one of the pillars of  islam  is to make a pilgrimage to mecca if you  can  jewish people go on pilgrimages to  jerusalem  and in the middle ages the whole of  europe was crisscrossed with  pilgrimage roots here in england by far  the most famous was the  pilgrimage to the shriners and thomas  beckett in canterbury cathedral  and chaucer's book the canterbury tales  uh one of the first books in  recognizable english  tell is about stories the pilgrims told  each other on their journey to  canterbury  they said it was written around 1390.  um another great place of pilgrimage in  england was the shrine of our lady  at wellsingham the black madonna of  wellsingham  the way in which this happened was that  people went on these journeys  they stayed in monasteries on the way  the monasteries provided the  infrastructure the places to sleep and  get food  for pilgrims on their journey but this  came to an end  in england and in other parts of  northern europe  through the protestant reformation the  protestant reformers didn't like  pilgrimage they thought they were too  catholic  um and also the puritans were worried  that  people were having too much of a nice  time on pilgrimages they were fun  um they complained about irregular  sexual activity and so forth  um so uh they  there was a desire to suppress them in  many  protestant countries and that happened  in england in 1538  with thomas cromwell's injunction  against pilgrimage  and at the same time as part of the  reformation here in england the  monasteries  were privatized the monks were driven  out  some were executed um some were just  pensioned off  and the monasteries were closed down all  over england and that destroyed the  infrastructure for pilgrimage similar  things happened in  in northern germany and in scandinavia  in the lutheran  protestant countries of scandinavia  well this suppression of pilgrimage i  think left a kind of vacuum in the soul  of  english people um which  was filled or replaced by  a secularized version of pilgrimage a  few generations later  under the name of tourism the english  invented tourism  first with the grand tour on the  continent which rich young men went on  and this involved visiting the great  sacred places  among other things like you know the  ruins of ancient rome and greece  saint peter's in rome um the great  cathedrals  of europe and so on  so tourism uh then spread  and and became more and more uh  widespread  in in the world um until  it's now or at least until recently  until the covid lockdown was a  multi-billion dollar industry all over  the world  and what do tourists go to do well they  usually go to see  ancient sacred places um terrorists  london visit westminster abbey and st  paul's cathedral  terrorists to you know cologne go to see  the cathedral  terrace to rome go to the vatican and  saint peter's  tourists to egypt go to the ancient  egyptian temples  tourists to india go to the great  temples and great mosques  and great religious tombs 